{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's a situation we see regularly on <a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond</a> properties:</p>

<p>Nice lake house, beautiful view, gutters working fine. But the basement stays damp after every storm. The homeowner has tried everything — longer downspout extensions, French drains along the foundation, even a sump pump. Nothing fully fixes it.</p>

<p>The problem is the lot. Lakefront properties pitch toward the water — that's why you have the view. But that pitch also means any water deposited within 15-20 feet of the house runs right back toward the foundation. Gravity doesn't care about your extensions. Water finds the lowest point.</p>

<p>Underground drains solve this by changing where the water enters the equation. Instead of dumping at the foundation and hoping it flows away, you pipe it underground to a discharge point that's already downhill from your house. The water never has a chance to flow back.</p>

<p>Same solution works for any property where surface grading works against you, or where clay soil prevents absorption. Scottsboro has plenty of both. The clay around here doesn't absorb water — it sheds it sideways. If you're dumping water 6 feet from your foundation on clay soil, you might as well dump it 2 feet away. Underground routing is the only way to genuinely move water far enough that it can't cause problems.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect your downspouts to a discharge point far from your foundation. Simple concept, but details matter.</p>

<p><strong>The pipe:</strong> We use 4" solid PVC for most residential applications. Some contractors use corrugated flexible pipe because it's cheaper and easier to install. Problem is, corrugated pipe clogs more easily (debris catches in the ridges) and can crush under soil pressure. Solid PVC costs a bit more but lasts essentially forever.</p>

<p><strong>The slope:</strong> Drains work by gravity, so the pipe needs to slope toward the discharge point. Minimum is 1/8" per foot (1% grade), but we prefer 1/4" per foot (2% grade) when elevation allows. More slope means faster drainage and less chance of debris accumulation.</p>

<p><strong>The connections:</strong> Each downspout connects via a catch basin that includes a debris basket. This is important — you don't want leaf debris entering the underground system. The baskets need to be checked and cleared annually, but that's easier than dealing with a clogged buried pipe.</p>

<p><strong>The discharge:</strong> Pop-up emitters release water at grade level when flow pressure builds up. Daylight outlets discharge onto slopes or into drainage areas. For <a href="/recent-projects/">lakefront properties</a>, we can sometimes route directly toward the water. The right option depends on your lot.</p>

<p>Installation means digging trenches (6-8" wide, 8-12" deep typically), laying pipe, making connections, and backfilling. A typical two-downspout system with 30-foot runs takes most of a day.</p>
{% endset %}

